\section{Introduction to Axiomatic Semantics}
\label{sec:axiomatic-intro}

While operational semantics describes \emph{how} programs execute and denotational semantics describes \emph{what} programs mean, axiomatic semantics takes a fundamentally different approach: it characterizes programs by the \emph{logical properties} they satisfy. Rather than defining the meaning of a program as a mathematical function or a sequence of state transitions, axiomatic semantics describes programs through assertions---logical formulas that hold at various points during execution.

The axiomatic approach was pioneered by Robert Floyd in 1967, who introduced the concept of verifying flowchart programs through assertions attached to arcs between nodes. Tony Hoare refined and generalized Floyd's method in 1969, producing the elegant system of proof rules that now bears his name. Edsger Dijkstra contributed the complementary notion of weakest preconditions, providing a calculational approach to program derivation and verification.

\begin{historical}
Floyd's 1967 paper ``Assigning Meanings to Programs'' introduced the idea of attaching assertions to the arcs of a flowchart and verifying that each arc preserves the truth of its assertion. Hoare's 1969 paper ``An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming'' presented the system of proof rules for a simple imperative language that became the foundation of program verification. Dijkstra's 1975 paper ``Guarded Commands, Nondeterminacy and Formal Derivation of Programs'' introduced the weakest precondition calculus as a tool for deriving programs from their specifications. These three works, together with the later contributions of Cook, Gries, and others, form the classical foundation of axiomatic semantics.
\end{historical}

The central insight of axiomatic semantics is that we can reason about programs without reference to their operational behavior. Instead, we work entirely within a logical framework, deriving properties of programs through the application of proof rules. This approach is particularly well suited to \emph{program verification}, where the goal is to prove that a program satisfies a formal specification.

\section{Hoare Triples}
\label{sec:hoare-triples}

The fundamental construct of axiomatic semantics is the \emph{Hoare triple}, a statement of the form:

\begin{definition}[Hoare Triple]
\label{def:hoare-triple}
A \textbf{Hoare triple} (or \textbf{Hoare formula}) is an expression of the form
\[
\{P\}\; S \;\{Q\}
\]
where $P$ and $Q$ are assertions (first-order logic formulas over program variables) and $S$ is a program statement. The triple asserts that if $P$ holds before execution of $S$, and $S$ terminates, then $Q$ holds after execution of $S$.
\end{definition}

The assertion $P$ is called the \textbf{precondition} and $Q$ is called the \textbf{postcondition}. We distinguish two interpretations of Hoare triples:

\begin{definition}[Partial Correctness]
\label{def:partial-correctness}
The Hoare triple $\{P\}\; S \;\{Q\}$ is \textbf{valid in the sense of partial correctness} (written $\models \{P\}\; S \;\{Q\}$) if and only if for every state $\sigma$ satisfying $P$, if the execution of $S$ starting from $\sigma$ terminates in a state $\sigma'$, then $\sigma'$ satisfies $Q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total Correctness]
\label{def:total-correctness}
The Hoare triple $\{P\}\; S \;\{Q\}$ is \textbf{valid in the sense of total correctness} (written $\models_{\mathrm{tot}} \{P\}\; S \;\{Q\}$) if and only if for every state $\sigma$ satisfying $P$, the execution of $S$ starting from $\sigma$ is guaranteed to terminate in a state $\sigma'$ that satisfies $Q$.
\end{definition}

The distinction between partial and total correctness is crucial. Partial correctness says nothing about termination: it only guarantees that \emph{if} the program terminates, the postcondition holds. Total correctness additionally guarantees that the program \emph{will} terminate. We use the notation $\models_{\mathrm{part}}$ for partial correctness and $\models_{\mathrm{tot}}$ for total correctness when the distinction matters.

\begin{example}[Partial vs.\ Total Correctness]
\label{ex:partial-total}
Consider the program $S \equiv \texttt{while } x > 0 \texttt{ do } x := x - 1$.

The triple $\{x \geq 0\}\; S \;\{x = 0\}$ is valid in the sense of total correctness: for any initial state where $x \geq 0$, the loop terminates and $x = 0$ holds.

The triple $\{\top\}\; S \;\{x = 0\}$ is valid in the sense of partial correctness: if the program terminates, then $x = 0$ holds. However, it is \emph{not} valid in the sense of total correctness, since the program does not terminate when $x < 0$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Assertions and States}
\label{sec:assertions-states}

An \emph{assertion} is a first-order logic formula over program variables. Formally, assertions are built from:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Arithmetic expressions: $e ::= x \mid n \mid e_1 + e_2 \mid e_1 - e_2 \mid e_1 \times e_2$
  \item Boolean expressions: $b ::= \top \mid \bot \mid e_1 = e_2 \mid e_1 < e_2 \mid b_1 \land b_2 \mid b_1 \lor b_2 \mid \lnot b$
  \item Quantified formulas: $\forall x.\, P$, $\exists x.\, P$
\end{itemize}

A state $\sigma$ (or \emph{store}) maps program variables to values. We write $\sigma \models P$ to indicate that assertion $P$ is true in state $\sigma$. The satisfaction relation is defined in the standard way for first-order logic.

\begin{definition}[Logical Implication of Assertions]
\label{def:assertion-implication}
We say that assertion $P$ \textbf{logically implies} assertion $Q$, written $P \Rightarrow Q$, if and only if for every state $\sigma$, $\sigma \models P$ implies $\sigma \models Q$.
\end{definition}

The rule of consequence, presented below, allows us to strengthen preconditions and weaken postconditions using logical implication.

\section{Proof Rules for Partial Correctness}
\label{sec:proof-rules-partial}

Hoare's proof system consists of a set of inference rules, one for each syntactic form of the language. We present the rules for a simple imperative language with the following syntax:

\begin{grammar}{Imperative Language for Hoare Logic}
  S & ::= x := e \altproduces S_1 ; S_2 \altproduces \textbf{if } B \textbf{ then } S_1 \textbf{ else } S_2 \\
    & \altproduces \textbf{while } B \textbf{ do } S \altproduces \textbf{skip}
\end{grammar}

\subsection{The Assignment Axiom}
\label{sec:assignment-axiom}

The axiom for assignment is the cornerstone of Hoare logic. It is remarkable in that it works \emph{backwards}: given a desired postcondition, it tells us what precondition must hold.

\begin{axiom}[Assignment]
\label{ax:assignment}
\[
\{P[e/x]\}\; x := e \;\{P\}
\]
where $P[e/x]$ denotes the assertion obtained by substituting every free occurrence of $x$ in $P$ with the expression $e$.
\end{axiom}

The intuition is straightforward: after executing $x := e$, the variable $x$ has the value that $e$ had before the assignment. Therefore, for $P$ to hold after the assignment, $P$ must hold with $e$ in place of $x$ before the assignment.

\begin{example}[Applying the Assignment Axiom]
\label{ex:assignment-axiom}
Consider the assignment $x := x + 1$ with postcondition $x > 0$. By the assignment axiom, the required precondition is:
\[
(x > 0)[x+1/x] \;=\; x + 1 > 0 \;=\; x > -1
\]
Thus $\{x > -1\}\; x := x + 1 \;\{x > 0\}$ is a valid Hoare triple.

For a more complex example, consider $x := x + y$ with postcondition $x = 2 \cdot y$. The precondition is:
\[
(x = 2 \cdot y)[x+y/x] \;=\; x + y = 2 \cdot y \;=\; x = y
\]
Thus $\{x = y\}\; x := x + y \;\{x = 2 \cdot y\}$.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Rule of Consequence}
\label{sec:rule-of-consequence}

The rule of consequence allows us to adjust preconditions and postconditions using logical implication:

\begin{semrules}{Rule of Consequence}
  \dfrac{P' \Rightarrow P \qquad \{P\}\; S \;\{Q\} \qquad Q \Rightarrow Q'}{\{P'\}\; S \;\{Q'\}}
\end{semrules}

This rule states that if we can prove $\{P\}\; S \;\{Q\}$, and $P'$ is stronger than $P$, and $Q$ is stronger than $Q'$, then $\{P'\}\; S \;\{Q'\}$ also holds.

\subsection{The Composition Rule}
\label{sec:composition-rule}

For sequential composition, the postcondition of the first statement becomes an intermediate assertion that serves as the precondition of the second:

\begin{semrules}{Composition}
  \dfrac{\{P\}\; S_1 \;\{R\} \qquad \{R\}\; S_2 \;\{Q\}}{\{P\}\; S_1 ; S_2 \;\{Q\}}
\end{semrules}

\begin{example}[Sequential Composition]
\label{ex:composition}
We wish to prove $\{x = 1\}\; x := x + 1 ;\; x := x \times 2 \;\{x = 4\}$.

Working backwards from the postcondition:
\begin{itemize}
  \item For $x := x \times 2$ with postcondition $x = 4$: precondition is $x \times 2 = 4$, i.e., $x = 2$.
  \item For $x := x + 1$ with postcondition $x = 2$: precondition is $x + 1 = 2$, i.e., $x = 1$.
\end{itemize}

The intermediate assertion is $R \equiv x = 2$. The proof is:
\[
\{x = 1\}\; x := x + 1 \;\{x = 2\} \qquad \{x = 2\}\; x := x \times 2 \;\{x = 4\}
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{The Conditional Rule}
\label{sec:conditional-rule}

For conditional statements, we must prove the postcondition under each branch, with the branch condition added to the precondition:

\begin{semrules}{Conditional}
  \dfrac{\{P \land B\}\; S_1 \;\{Q\} \qquad \{P \land \lnot B\}\; S_2 \;\{Q\}}{\{P\}\; \textbf{if } B \textbf{ then } S_1 \textbf{ else } S_2 \;\{Q\}}
\end{semrules}

When the else branch is $\textbf{skip}$, the rule simplifies to:

\begin{semrules}{Conditional without Else}
  \dfrac{\{P \land B\}\; S \;\{Q\} \qquad P \land \lnot B \Rightarrow Q}{\{P\}\; \textbf{if } B \textbf{ then } S \;\{Q\}}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{The Skip Axiom}
\label{sec:skip-axiom}

The skip statement does not change the state, so any assertion that holds before skip also holds after:

\begin{axiom}[Skip]
\label{ax:skip}
\[
\{P\}\; \textbf{skip} \;\{P\}
\]
\end{axiom}

This is a special case of the assignment axiom, since $\textbf{skip}$ is equivalent to assigning each variable to itself.

\section{Loop Invariants and the While Rule}
\label{sec:loop-invariants}

The while rule is the most challenging rule in Hoare logic because it requires finding a \emph{loop invariant}---an assertion that is preserved by every iteration of the loop.

\begin{semrules}{While Rule (Partial Correctness)}
  \dfrac{\{I \land B\}\; S \;\{I\}}{\{I\}\; \textbf{while } B \textbf{ do } S \;\{I \land \lnot B\}}
\end{semrules}

The assertion $I$ is the \textbf{loop invariant}. The rule states that if $I$ is preserved by one iteration of the loop body (when the condition $B$ holds), then $I$ is preserved by any number of iterations. When the loop terminates, both $I$ and $\lnot B$ hold.

\begin{definition}[Loop Invariant]
\label{def:loop-invariant}
An assertion $I$ is a \textbf{loop invariant} for $\textbf{while } B \textbf{ do } S$ if and only if $\{I \land B\}\; S \;\{I\}$ is valid.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Loop Invariant for Summation]
\label{ex:loop-invariant-sum}
Consider the program that computes the sum of the first $n$ natural numbers:
\begin{verbatim}
  s := 0;
  i := 1;
  while i <= n do
    s := s + i;
    i := i + 1
\end{verbatim}

We claim the postcondition $s = n(n+1)/2$. The appropriate loop invariant is:
\[
I \equiv s = \frac{i(i-1)}{2} \land 1 \leq i \leq n + 1
\]

Verification:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Initialization:} After $s := 0; i := 1$, we have $s = 0 = 1 \cdot 0 / 2$ and $i = 1$, so $I$ holds.
  \item \textbf{Preservation:} Assuming $I \land i \leq n$, after $s := s + i; i := i + 1$:
    \[
    s' = s + i = \frac{i(i-1)}{2} + i = \frac{i(i+1)}{2} = \frac{(i+1) \cdot i}{2}
    \]
    and $i' = i + 1$, so $s' = i'(i'-1)/2$, confirming $I$.
  \item \textbf{Postcondition:} When $I \land i > n$ holds, since $i \leq n+1$ (from $I$) and $i > n$, we have $i = n+1$. Thus $s = (n+1)n/2 = n(n+1)/2$.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Finding Loop Invariants}
\label{sec:finding-invariants}

Finding appropriate loop invariants is often the most difficult part of program verification. Several strategies can guide the search:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Generalization:} Replace constants in the postcondition with variables that track the loop's progress. In the summation example, $n$ is replaced by $i$.
  \item \textbf{Accumulation properties:} For loops that accumulate values, the invariant typically relates the accumulator to a function of the loop counter.
  \item \textbf{Bounds:} Loop invariants often include bounds on loop variables, derived from the loop condition and the update.
  \item \textbf{Data structure invariants:} For loops manipulating data structures, the invariant captures structural properties (e.g., sortedness of an array prefix).
\end{itemize}

\section{Variant Functions and Total Correctness}
\label{sec:variant-functions}

To prove total correctness of loops, we must additionally show that the loop terminates. This requires a \emph{variant function} (also called a \emph{bound function} or \emph{decreasing measure}).

\begin{definition}[Variant Function]
\label{def:variant-function}
A \textbf{variant function} for a loop $\textbf{while } B \textbf{ do } S$ with invariant $I$ is an integer-valued expression $V$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $I \land B \Rightarrow V > 0$ (the variant is positive when the loop continues)
  \item $\{I \land B \land V = v_0\}\; S \;\{V < v_0\}$ (the variant strictly decreases with each iteration)
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{semrules}{While Rule (Total Correctness)}
  \dfrac{
    \{I \land B \land V = v_0\}\; S \;\{I \land V < v_0\} \qquad
    I \land B \Rightarrow V > 0
  }{
    \{I\}\; \textbf{while } B \textbf{ do } S \;\{I \land \lnot B\}
  }
  \quad \text{($v_0$ not free in $I$, $B$, or $S$)}
\end{semrules}

\begin{example}[Variant Function for Summation]
\label{ex:variant-sum}
For the summation loop of Example~\ref{ex:loop-invariant-sum}, the variant function is $V = n - i + 1$. We verify:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $I \land i \leq n \Rightarrow n - i + 1 \geq 1 > 0$.
  \item After $i := i + 1$, the new value of $V$ is $n - (i+1) + 1 = n - i = V - 1 < V$.
\end{itemize}
Thus the loop terminates, and the total correctness triple $\{n \geq 0\}\; \text{loop} \;\{s = n(n+1)/2\}$ is established.
\end{example}

